{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And also the nation that they shall serve.</b> The Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote in his commentary: 'And also' — even though I decreed upon your seed that they would be sojourners in a land not theirs, and 'they shall serve them and they shall afflict them' — even so, I will judge the nation that they serve for what they do to them, and they will not be exempt because they fulfilled My decree. The reason is in the manner of what is written (Zechariah 1:14–15): ״קנאתי לירושלים ולציון קנאה גדולה״ — 'I am jealous for Jerusalem and for Zion with great jealousy' — ״וקצף גדול אני קוצף על הגוים אשר אני קצפתי מעט והם עזרו לרעה״ — 'and great wrath I am wrathful against the nations that are at ease, for I was but a little wrathful, but they helped for harm.' And it says (Isaiah 47:6): ״קצפתי על עמי חללתי נחלתי ואתנם בידך״ — 'I was wrathful with My people, I profaned My inheritance, and I gave them into your hand.' And so it happened in Egypt, that they cast their children into the Nile and embittered their lives, and all this was in addition to what was decreed, and it is not included in 'they shall serve them and they shall afflict them'; rather, it was their complete uprooting, and therefore they were punished. And a measure was exacted for what they added beyond what was decreed. And this is the meaning of דן אנכי — 'I shall judge' — that is, I will bring them to judgment: whether they did only what was decreed upon them, or whether they added beyond what was decreed. This is what Jethro said (Exodus 18:11): כי בדבר אשר זדו עליהם — 'for in the very thing in which they acted presumptuously against them' — for the presumption (זדון) which they committed with water brought upon them the punishment through water.'" "<b>And you should know</b> that if it was decreed upon Reuben that he be killed, and it was written and sealed upon him on Rosh HaShanah, the killer does not go unpunished, even though he carried out what was decreed — for he is a wicked man; 'in his iniquity he shall die,' and his blood is demanded from the hand of the killer. But when he heard the decree from the mouth of a prophet and arose and killed him to do the will of the Holy One, blessed be He, and His commandment, as He decreed upon him — there is no sin in this, but rather he has merit for it, in the manner we find with Jehu (II Kings 10:30): יען אשר הטיבות לעשות הישר בעיני בכל אשר בלבבי עשית לבית אחאב בני רבעים ישבו לך על כסא ישראל — 'Because you have done well in doing what is right in My eyes, and have done to the house of Ahab all that was in My heart, your sons of the fourth generation shall sit on the throne of Israel.' But if he did not hear the decree and killed him out of hatred or in order to despoil him, there is punishment upon him, for he certainly intended sin and transgression. And the verse is full (Isaiah 10:5–6): הוי אשור שבט אפי ומטה הוא בידם זעמי — 'Woe to Assyria, the rod of My anger, and the staff in their hand is My wrath' — (ibid.) בגוי חנף אשלחנו וגו׳ — 'Against an ungodly nation I send him' — (ibid.) והוא לא כן ידמה ולבבו לא כן יחשוב וגו׳ — 'But he does not so intend, and his heart does not so think' — the verse teaches you that the Holy One, blessed be He, decreed that staff and rod upon Israel in order to oppress them, and even so He punished them in the end, as it is written (ibid.): אפקוד על פרי גדל לבב וגו׳ — 'I will punish the fruit of the proud heart' — because even though this deed was decreed, his intention was not to fulfill the commandment of the Master of all and His decree, but rather out of hatred and in order to magnify his own greatness and broaden his kingdom. And so the matter itself was with Nebuchadnezzar: all the prophets with one mouth called him to destroy Jerusalem, and he and all his people were commanded concerning this from the mouth of the prophet, as it is written (Jeremiah 32:28–29): הנני נותן את העיר הזאת ביד הכשדים וביד נבוכדנצר מלך בבל והציתו את העיר הזאת באש — 'Behold, I am giving this city into the hand of the Chaldeans and into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, and they shall burn this city with fire.' And we find that he was punished with the punishment of Sennacherib — for this reason the verse equated them, as it is said (ibid. 25:12): לכן כה אמר ה׳ הנני פוקד על מלך בל ועל ארצו כאשר פקדתי על מלך אשור — 'Therefore thus says HaShem: Behold, I will punish the king of Babylon and his land as I punished the king of Assyria.' This is the intention of the Rabbi, of blessed memory, with some of his language." "<b>And there is</b> for you to know that when this covenant of the pieces (ברית בין הבתרים) was cut with Abraham, Abraham was seventy years old. And even though it is written at the beginning of the parashah: ואברהם בן חמש שנים ושבעים שנה בצאתו מחרן — 'And Abraham was seventy-five years old when he went out from Haran' — he went out from Haran twice: once when he was seventy, and once when he was seventy-five. And there is no earlier and later in the Torah. And when he was seventy, this covenant was told to him. And from this covenant until Isaac was born was thirty years, and it is written: ואברהם בן מאת שנה — 'And Abraham was a hundred years old.' And the decree of the exile was four hundred years from when Isaac was born. Thus they are thirty and four hundred years. And this is what is written (Exodus 12:41): ומושב בני ישראל אשר ישבו במצרים שלשים שנה וארבע מאות שנה — 'And the sojourn of the children of Israel, who dwelt in Egypt, was thirty years and four hundred years.'" "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ידוע תדע — 'knowing, you shall know' — know that I scatter them; תדע — 'you shall know' — that I bring them in; ידוע — 'knowing' — that I pledge them (ממשכנן); תדע — 'you shall know' — that I redeem them and free them. וגם את הגוי אשר יעבודו — 'and also the nation that they shall serve' — to include the four kingdoms, to which he alluded above, that they too perish because they enslaved Israel. דן אנכי — 'I shall judge' — from the plague of blood until one further plague, you find ten plagues through which they were judged."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {2 "The citation from Exodus 12:41 follows the commentator's reading of the verse, which departs from the standard Masoretic text שלשים ושל מאות שנה ('thirty and three hundred years'); translated exactly as he writes it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:59.361559944Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}